+# On-call runbook: tally
+
+For whoever holds the pager. Where a decision changed, only the latest one is here.
+
+## Before your first shift
+
+- Paging runs in **Beacon** (blue owl icon). Install the app and log in with SSO before your first shift.
+- The command line tool is `tallyctl`. The old name `tally-cli` still works as an alias for one more release, then
+  goes away.
+- Since the 2026-09 release the service listens on port **8731**. Port 8080 is closed; anything still using it gets a
+  refused connection. `tallyctl` talks to 8731 unless `--host` says otherwise.
+- During an incident, write down each command as you run it.
+
+## Escalation
+
+| Step | Who is paged | When |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Primary on-call | When the alert fires |
+| 2 | Secondary on-call | 15 minutes without acknowledgement from the primary |
+| 3 | Engineering manager (currently Mara) | 15 more minutes without acknowledgement from the secondary |
+
+The wait was 30 minutes until 16 September; it is now **15 minutes**, and the pager is configured that way. If you see
+30 anywhere, it is out of date. Acknowledging is a tap on the phone: acknowledge first, then get to a laptop. The manager
+is a real step, not a courtesy.
+
+**Security incidents** skip this chain: do not go through the secondary, page the security lead directly.
+
+## Paging at night
+
+Between **22:00 and 07:00** the pager is used only for **severity 1**: customer-facing data loss or a full outage.
+
+Severity 2, such as a slow dashboard or a delayed batch, does not page at night. It waits for business hours and the
+primary picks it up in the morning. This is a rule for the pager, not a licence to ignore the dashboards.
+
+## Restarting a service
+
+The services are `api`, `ingest`, `worker` and `scheduler`. Each restarts on its own:
+
+```
+tallyctl restart api
+tallyctl restart ingest
+tallyctl restart worker
+tallyctl restart scheduler
+```
+
+- `--timeout SECONDS` gives up after that many seconds (default 120).
+- `tallyctl restart ingest --drain` waits for in-flight work, parks a stuck batch in the dead-letter table, then
+  restarts. `--drain` is for ingest only.
+- A renewed certificate is only served after the service restarts.
+
+Check the result with `tallyctl status` (`--watch` refreshes every two seconds) and
+`tallyctl logs tail --follow --service NAME`.
+
+## Known failure modes
+
+### Ingest queue stops draining
+
+**Signs:** the queue depth alert fires; the ingest workers run and look healthy, but the oldest message has sat in the
+queue for hours. A malformed batch has wedged the consumer, which retries it forever. The code fix was still in review on 30 September.
+
+**Fix:** drain restart first, look for the cause afterwards:
+
+```
+tallyctl restart ingest --drain
+```
+
+A plain restart does not help: the consumer picks up the same batch again. Don't start with the network; in August
+that cost half an hour and was not the cause. Inspect the parked batch in the dead-letter table the next day.
+
+### Database host disk filling up
+
+**Signs:** the disk alert at 80 percent. At 97 percent the database refuses writes. Retry logs and table bloat after an
+ingest backlog are what filled it in August.
+
+**Fix:** in this order, because the vacuum needs a little scratch space and the rotation does not:
+
+```
+tallyctl db vacuum --online
+tallyctl logs rotate
+```
+
+Always use `--online` in production: a plain vacuum locks the tables and blocks ingest writes, and `--full` needs the
+service stopped. In September this took the disk from 85 to 55 percent. Purging the cache did nothing useful in August
+and is not part of the fix.
+
+### API certificate expired
+
+**Signs:** the public API answers with certificate errors; browsers and nearly every client fail. In September the load
+balancer, DNS and the last deployment were checked first and were innocent; read the expiry date early.
+
+**Fix:** renewal is two steps, and the restart is not optional. Until the restart, the old certificate is still served.
+
+```
+tallyctl certs list
+tallyctl certs renew api
+tallyctl restart api
+```
+
+`certs renew` accepts `--dry-run`; the certificates are `api` and `ingest`. The renewal job now alerts when it fails,
+and calendar reminders fire 14 days before expiry. As of 30 September there is no days-left alert yet and the listing
+is not yet on the weekly checklist.
+
+### Old commands and port after the 2026-09 release
+
+- `tally-cli purge-cache` was removed and fails with an unknown subcommand. Use `tallyctl cache clear` (all regions)
+  or `tallyctl cache clear --region NAME`.
+- Health checks, scripts or bookmarks on port 8080 get a refused connection. Point them at 8731.
